    "slug": "ethiopia-tells-un-security-council-it-will-defend-itself-accuses-eritrea-of-incursion",
    "summary": "Ethiopia has informed the UN Security Council that it will take measures to defend its territory, accusing Eritrea of sending troops into northern Ethiopia and seizing areas in Tigray region.",
    "content": "Ethiopia has formally notified the United Nations Security Council of its intention to take defensive actions, according to a letter from Ethiopian Foreign Minister Gedion Timothewos. In the letter, Ethiopia accuses Eritrea of deploying forces into northern Ethiopia and capturing parts of the Tigray region. The communication cites Article 51 of the UN Charter, which affirms the right of states to self-defense against armed attack, but does not specify what military steps Ethiopia might take.\n\nThe accusation marks a serious escalation in the long-troubled relationship between Ethiopia and Eritrea. The two neighbors fought a devastating border war from 1998 to 2000, which ended with a peace agreement but left unresolved tensions. In 2018, a historic peace deal was signed, raising hopes for reconciliation. However, relations have deteriorated again in recent years, particularly amid the conflict in Tigray that began in 2020. Eritrean forces were involved in that conflict, fighting alongside Ethiopian federal forces against Tigrayan forces, but their presence was often denied.\n\nThis latest development comes after months of rising friction. Eritrea has repeatedly denied involvement in Tigray since the 2022 peace agreement that ended major fighting there. Ethiopia's letter to the Security Council suggests that Addis Ababa now believes Eritrea is acting aggressively, potentially destabilizing the already fragile post-war situation in Tigray. The international community has yet to respond publicly to Ethiopia's claims.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly concerning. Many Somalis have strong cultural, historical, and economic ties to the Horn of Africa region. Ethiopia is a key player in Somali affairs, contributing troops to the African Union mission in Somalia (ATMIS) and hosting large numbers of Somali refugees. A conflict between Ethiopia and Eritrea could divert attention and resources away from Somalia's own security challenges, including the fight against al-Shabaab. Additionally, any instability in the region risks disrupting trade routes and remittance flows that are lifelines for many Somali families.\n\nSomali communities in the diaspora, especially in Europe, North America, and the Middle East, are closely watching these events. They have long been affected by conflicts in the Horn, and any new outbreak of hostilities could lead to further displacement and humanitarian needs. It may also complicate ongoing efforts to stabilize Somalia and the broader region. Diaspora organizations often act as informal diplomats and advocates, and they may now urge international partners to de-escalate tensions between Ethiopia and Eritrea.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/11/itoobiya-oo-qaramada-midoobay-u-sheegtay-inay-isdifaacayso-eritrea-na-ku-eedeysay-inay-dhulkeeda-soo-gashay/",
